Mathematics has not previously been solved by AI and AI systems less capable than current systems with natural language generations capabilities, due to a number of factors including understandability and available training data.

The problem requires development of AI capabilities distinct from those currently in AI systems, including:
1. Reading, understanding, and constructing novel mathematical proofs from scratch.
2. Proposing entirely new mathematical concepts and implementations to attack previously unknown, open problems.
3. Proof verification by a human conference track.
4. Acceptance of that proof by a consensus of top-tier researchers.

The first two capabilities seem very difficult to achieve, and have not shown much progress towards that goal, which means that it is difficult to imagine successful outcomes where AI contribution provides substantial advances toward solving a Millennium Prize problem.
The problems also involve original investigations, which is exploratory in purpose and goal.
Attempts by AI include reformulating the problem and attempting to prove it for special cases, but without breakthrough results. Translations of the completed proofs to current-day mathematical language may be accomplished reading proofs surreptitiously by deductive logic.user
